WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.433
BERT.

2
00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:07.133
A model that learns language by filling in blanks, and quietly powers many systems you will build.

3
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.833
BERT, from 2018, is an encoder-only transformer.

4
00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:15.567
It is pretrained by hiding fifteen percent of the tokens.

5
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:19.700
Then it predicts them using context from both the left and the right.

6
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.167
Here is a sentence with one word hidden.

7
00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:29.267
To fill the blank, the mask looks at words on both sides: sudden before it, tank and pressure after it.

8
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:32.167
BERT started the fine-tune era.

9
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:34.267
You download the pretrained weights.

10
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.000
Add a small head on top.

11
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.200
And train briefly on your own labels.

12
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.367
Let's try it.

13
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:43.233
We load bert base with the fill-mask pipeline.

14
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:45.891
Then we print each guess with its score.

15
00:00:45.891 --> 00:00:48.602
The top guesses are drop, increase, and spike.

16
00:00:48.602 --> 00:00:50.900
That's the pretraining objective, live.

17
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.767
Don't file BERT under history.

18
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:57.300
Embedding models are BERT-family encoders with pooling.

19
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.033
When a job says embeddings or reranking, it is saying BERT.

20
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:04.267
Here is where it lives in a RAG system.

21
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:07.567
Vector search narrows forty thousand chunks to fifty.

22
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:11.633
A cross-encoder, a BERT, reads the query and each chunk together.

23
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:14.108
It is slower, so it only sees fifty.

24
00:01:14.108 --> 00:01:18.700
But it catches chunks that match every term yet answer a different question.

25
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:20.682
Let's recap.

26
00:01:20.682 --> 00:01:24.200
BERT is encoder-only, trained to fill in masked words.

27
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.433
It reads context from both sides.

28
00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:28.500
You fine-tune it with a small head.

29
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.691
And it lives on as embedders and rerankers.

30
00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:32.833
Next up: RoBERTa.
